Atopic Dermatitis
Paediatric & Neonatal
Intense pruritus, Flexural excoriated plaques, Severe skin dryness (xerosis)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-006
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of intensely pruritic skin lesions causing profound sleep deprivation and urgent need to rule out superimposed bacterial infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a highly distressed child with extensive weeping skin lesions and two nights of zero sleep for the entire household. Immediate after-hours assessment was required to assess for secondary bacterial impetiginization and to provide immediate targeted therapy to prevent further excoriation and cutaneous barrier breakdown prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They have scratched their arms and legs raw, and they haven't slept in two days because of the severe itching."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a chronic, relapsing inflammatory skin disease driven by epidermal barrier dysfunction, which acutely flared 48 hours ago. The exacerbation began with pronounced generalized skin dryness that rapidly evolved into intensely pruritic patches. Over the last 24 hours, relentless chronic rubbing and scratching have led to excoriated, weeping plaques localized to the antecubital and popliteal flexural creases. There is a strong immediate family history of asthma and allergic rhinitis.
Functional Status: Highly agitated, severely sleep-deprived.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme caregiver fatigue; high risk of secondary skin infection due to unremitting scratching.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 110 | Temp: 37.1C | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 20 Kg
General: Fatigued, constantly scratching extremities, visually distressed but alert.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals severe skin dryness (xerosis) globally. There are flexural excoriated plaques in the antecubital and popliteal fossae with localized erythema and lichenification. Intense pruritus is clinically evident. Dennie-Morgan lines are prominent infraorbitally. Mild keratosis pilaris is noted on the lateral upper arms. There is no honey-colored crusting or fluctuance to suggest frank bacterial impetiginization.
Environmental Observations: Overheated room, heavily perfumed laundry detergent noted in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Over-the-counter hydrocortisone 1% applied with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Atopic Dermatitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Scabies - Ruled out due to the absence of burrows in the interdigital web spaces and lack of nocturnal worsening specifically out of proportion to daytime itching.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out due to the chronic, relapsing nature of the presentation and classic flexural distribution rather than a sharply demarcated exposure pattern.

Psoriasis - Ruled out due to the absence of well-demarcated erythematous plaques with silvery scale on extensor surfaces.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s acute presentation of unrelenting, severe pruritus and extensive cutaneous excoriation mandated an urgent after-hours physical assessment. Telephone triage recognized the high risk of secondary bacterial infection and the severe impact of acute sleep deprivation on the child and caregivers. Objective findings confirmed a severe flare of atopic dermatitis without superimposed impetigo. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in continued skin trauma, inevitable bacterial superinfection, and intolerable distress. Immediate intervention provided potent topical corticosteroids and systemic antihistamines to rapidly restore the epidermal barrier and facilitate sleep.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment BD for 5 days to active flares. Recommended oral Promethazine 10mg stat tonight for sedation and itch relief.
Positive Behavioral Change: Immediately provide a high-value sticker or small token specifically when the child allows the application of thick emollient immediately after bathing without scratching or resisting. This direct, positive reinforcement establishes emollient application as a rewarding routine rather than a punitive medical task.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None tonight.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provide cotton sleep gloves.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to monitor response to topical steroids.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a rapidly spreading red rash, honey-colored crusting, facial swelling, or an unexplained high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; chronic skin conditions require ongoing management and are subject to frequent relapses.
Clinical Course: The acute flare and intense itching should improve within 48 hours of compliant topical steroid application.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the skin becomes hot, exquisitely tender, or develops spreading yellow crusts.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The steroid ointment directly suppresses the severe skin inflammation, while the oral antihistamine will induce sleep and block the reflex to scratch.
Actionable Steps: Apply steroids strictly to the red, inflamed areas only. Apply a thick layer of plain, unperfumed moisturizer all over the body immediately after a lukewarm, short bath.

SMS INFORMATION

